[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "300e195c-ddeb-57cf-a4bf-c0e974317b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "300e195c-ddeb-57cf-a4bf-c0e974317b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317710} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b62001e-c670-5582-880c-5e2e4169c1ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71f329b9-d17b-5f51-b752-6c65f97ea24c" :question/math-academy-id "q-345223" :question/problem "A body of mass $\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ meters per second. \n\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ seconds. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the force equals\n{{field-1}} $\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =ma(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F(\\frac{\\pi }{4})|=|2|=2\\,\\text{N}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319004 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39bb6fd0-ab83-5955-9da3-4caab22d47c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39bb6fd0-ab83-5955-9da3-4caab22d47c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317751} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9cb0d01-55ea-5aaa-a85d-5bfb232facd4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4258571-e2ee-5711-b11c-465f2f2d9827" :question/math-academy-id "q-345321" :question/problem "![](images/21/2102626f331aaa7803462f8e427ef2c0150deb554955199f3d402123934e815a.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+3$ and the $x$-axis between $x=-1$ and $x=1$, as shown above. Find the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+3x){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{2}{3}+3)-(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{3}-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{12}-(-\\frac{25}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319008 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ee21fdb-4f31-5662-8173-652df2970035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ee21fdb-4f31-5662-8173-652df2970035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317766} :answer-field/id #uuid "43d3cb79-dfa8-57a8-8ad1-d8a7c894979b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a5942b5-b345-55e9-98fa-6a0c078bad31" :question/math-academy-id "q-345340" :question/problem "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-1}] \\\\  & =-{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319011 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "418f9337-5384-52da-981d-31d9661f516d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "418f9337-5384-52da-981d-31d9661f516d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317777} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbf3ec70-b28b-5016-ba4e-0a5a05b82943" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "af180c34-7e0c-5b7b-9e5b-5a6251599f15" :question/math-academy-id "q-345381" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dce89dbea62566c0ee897bffe0638458970799bafdc7d7c8e5084328fef38bef.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}$, the $x$-axis, and the lines $x=-1$ and $x=1$, as shown above. Find the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-arcsin\\,(\\frac{x}{2}){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-(arcsin\\,(\\frac{1}{2})-arcsin\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is \n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62a7906c-2a37-5849-b484-c4001173664d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62a7906c-2a37-5849-b484-c4001173664d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317901} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cba2d9b-a343-5b67-885f-6b596a66cb05" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ac6cc325-8109-5df6-a8fa-f84e9e1ea235" :question/math-academy-id "q-345385" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d43d7419f631b479478641ac203c6816693c68ffc40ccc1d4a8a72da0a03461a.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}$, the $x$-axis, and the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, as shown above. Find the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =arccos\\,(\\frac{x}{3}){|}_{0}^{3/2} \\\\  & =arccos\\,(\\frac{1}{2})-arccos\\,(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is \n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "193fe597-715a-5d01-af1c-71fb91a8c26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "193fe597-715a-5d01-af1c-71fb91a8c26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317646} :answer-field/id #uuid "4861563e-97fd-5b25-8865-eb95ee9e7cf1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318583} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daf8cfa9-5ea5-5eb9-b8b4-6187321a94fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daf8cfa9-5ea5-5eb9-b8b4-6187321a94fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318297} :answer-field/id #uuid "7291b310-c1df-5538-adaf-946d11b24c06" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc894b99-a438-5f57-9099-db4cf3a5ce6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-345502" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ In **ascending order**, the **exact values** of the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})+n\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aace116-779e-5cd7-87fe-2a773103e41b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2aace116-779e-5cd7-87fe-2a773103e41b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317697} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1fe8d6b-b674-5235-b645-ef3a0cb2483c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af8d7c5c-930c-59d3-b49e-ff7666f63383" :question/math-academy-id "q-346081" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\frac{\\pi x}{4},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60f9d185-78b6-59cc-a36a-404564ab8739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60f9d185-78b6-59cc-a36a-404564ab8739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317893} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b52d148-4c11-50af-96f7-2d509700ed26" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58a433e2-084f-559f-bd64-1862ef8ffdc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-346084" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =0-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5ed63f3-57cc-59c7-b8f7-81f7530ff3ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5ed63f3-57cc-59c7-b8f7-81f7530ff3ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318217} :answer-field/id #uuid "f305be5a-3143-5d6a-b028-5ceb30c2d005" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8ad5354f-415f-5373-a369-188a90efa535" :question/math-academy-id "q-346090" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =cot\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-cot\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319067 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd55a046-ddb4-56de-ab65-a51210d86e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd55a046-ddb4-56de-ab65-a51210d86e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318246} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa47e58e-1210-5f7d-ae4f-2c6efa1bc040" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f3de3131-f650-58c5-a909-7a85b83b3f4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-346092" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-(-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "769b09c3-8e5a-5e55-99cd-e126e57155aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}-2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "769b09c3-8e5a-5e55-99cd-e126e57155aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}-2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317972} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ff5d4bc-02ea-5674-92d8-9925921dbed3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a00832dd-966d-5a5a-8da4-9f3ee1f87bfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-346094" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}-(-2) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}-2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319070 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca8bcc6d-5e6e-5f23-9931-9ba109153e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca8bcc6d-5e6e-5f23-9931-9ba109153e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318235} :answer-field/id #uuid "0de184e0-4c2b-55bb-bae9-b8061c599b03" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318456} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f64ae7e0-64b1-5f21-9e91-7ee063a78db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f64ae7e0-64b1-5f21-9e91-7ee063a78db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318378} :answer-field/id #uuid "92bf9fa3-b3c5-557c-b2c0-e0fba9edb465" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18197a01-d08b-5472-bcd0-342b90f62fdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-346179" :question/problem "Express ${\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{3}+2($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x+1,$ the lower bound is $a=0,$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(\\frac{2i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[({(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}\\,)}^{3}+2(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}\\,)+1)\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33e27707-d1a6-54e9-95db-6c0e39ed6676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33e27707-d1a6-54e9-95db-6c0e39ed6676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317731} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d25b445-440a-5f14-80c7-7f5d50d92fbe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e8c498fe-e615-5851-9e66-78de56e7b50e" :question/math-academy-id "q-346181" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{6}^{-3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{113}{9},$ calculate ${\\int }_{-3}^{6}(9f(x)+8x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{6}(9f(x)+8x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{6}^{-3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{113}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}(9f(x)+8x)\\,\\text{d}x & =9{\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,8{\\int }_{-3}^{6}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9(-\\frac{113}{9})\\,+\\,8\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{-3}^{6} \\\\  & =-113\\,+\\,8\\cdot \\frac{{6}^{2}-(-3)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-113\\,+\\,8\\cdot \\frac{27}{2} \\\\  & =-113\\,+\\,108 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b3a545a-f6fc-56d8-b618-306dd6c9b24f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{3i}{n}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b3a545a-f6fc-56d8-b618-306dd6c9b24f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{3i}{n}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317656} :answer-field/id #uuid "2169b17d-9b76-5e27-8f32-b49b8393f666" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76bb0795-0840-56ba-91a7-84e2ebc07a76" :question/math-academy-id "q-346217" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+1},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{3i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{(\\frac{3i}{n})+1}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{3i}{n}+1}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319089 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "869b8c29-ad99-5c1d-bdd5-cb1ad192a214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "869b8c29-ad99-5c1d-bdd5-cb1ad192a214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318022} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ee5e5c7-596a-5614-bb03-ce5ccba93bfb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "75f6ce5d-5587-5359-99a4-e02f7c9a6617" :question/math-academy-id "q-346240" :question/problem "A contractor has $18\\,in$ of trim to mark out a rectangular opening. Find the maximum area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$ that can be marked out with the trim. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,{in}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectagular area.\n\n![](images/59/59c6b4e09015898c1cd77d96fba2bc0dca037ef7462a15f90765257888e3331d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $18.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\n2x+2y=18.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & =18 \\\\ y & =9-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=9-x$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =x(9-x) \\\\ A & =9x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=9-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 9-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =9 \\\\ x & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=\\frac{9}{2}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(x)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{9}{2})=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{9}{2})$ is negative, we conclude that $x=\\frac{9}{2}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=\\frac{9}{2}$, we have \n\n$$\ny=9-\\frac{9}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=\\frac{9}{2}\\,in$ and $y=\\frac{9}{2}\\,in,$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{max}}=xy=\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}=20.25\\,{in}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319092 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c49f865-99de-519b-9b0e-6f73589c8822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c49f865-99de-519b-9b0e-6f73589c8822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317611} :answer-field/id #uuid "f048a17c-f1b2-56a1-8a31-975762ef6ad9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f33e4009-fc1f-57ea-a385-f971677ad6e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-346255" :question/problem "A farmer wants to build a rectangular livestock pen of area $648\\,{ft}^{2}.$ One side of the pen is adjacent to a barn, so that side does not require fencing. Find $x+y$ where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the pen that minimize the amount of fencing required.\n\n$x+y=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](images/83/8377f61340020a602e6226932a14fb56ceb803bc9efff05223682f9cca1cf91e.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=x+2y.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $648\\,{ft}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=648.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{648}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =x+2(\\frac{648}{x}) \\\\  & =x+\\frac{1296}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{1296}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{1296}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1296 \\\\ x & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=36$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{2592}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(36)=\\frac{2592}{(36)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{18}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(36)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=36$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{648}{36}=18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the lot that has the least perimeter are $x=36\\,ft$ and $y=18\\,ft,$ and we conclude that \n\n$$\nx+y=36+18=54\\,ft.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bef11467-f6f1-5099-a450-87b5379d2b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bef11467-f6f1-5099-a450-87b5379d2b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318198} :answer-field/id #uuid "8be0ff7c-611b-584d-b02c-0798a35d84aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "970cce65-2ceb-5bbc-b96e-29b0275ee6d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-346256" :question/problem "A park wants to build a rectangular skating area of area $1250\\,{yd}^{2}.$ One side of the area is adjacent to a wall, so that side does not require fencing. Find $x+y$ where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the area that minimize the amount of fencing required.\n\n$x+y=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{yd}$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](images/f1/f1ce707b311c320fe7dbf3ba2ad1daff6669a67ab1d7652fc8d98065c9ae747d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=x+2y.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $1250\\,{yd}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=1250.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1250}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =x+2(\\frac{1250}{x}) \\\\  & =x+\\frac{2500}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{2500}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{2500}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2500 \\\\ x & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=50$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{5000}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(50)=\\frac{5000}{(50)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{25}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(50)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=50$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=50,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1250}{50}=25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the lot that has the least perimeter are $x=50\\,yd$ and $y=25\\,yd,$ and we conclude that \n\n$$\nx+y=50+25=75\\,yd.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319094 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5eb82788-efbe-50de-b226-6bde5d6aedf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5eb82788-efbe-50de-b226-6bde5d6aedf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317886} :answer-field/id #uuid "47ffe2b0-f398-575b-afe8-d12d64c3f4fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec7ce12f-cbf4-5c03-8053-383589db4d45" :question/math-academy-id "q-346368" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\frac{x}{4},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $2\\pi /3$ | $2\\pi $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /6$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(u)\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u))\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =4(-1+2) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a18383d3-63cd-5639-931d-a7a1c504ecad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318118} {:answer/id #uuid "eef5da2c-2eb9-5619-b5fc-8c0b83845f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eef5da2c-2eb9-5619-b5fc-8c0b83845f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527772} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4db02f7-5977-595a-8560-ebd6520e3bc1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "684a7fc3-f685-5be8-90e1-52ef15ddd822" :question/math-academy-id "q-346458" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{100-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{100-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =6{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{100\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/100)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{10}{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/100}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/10)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{10})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{10}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{10}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=10\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{100-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/10)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 10\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "578ea0f9-6e27-559f-8d20-f7994570e05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "578ea0f9-6e27-559f-8d20-f7994570e05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317858} :answer-field/id #uuid "35bbb5d1-0990-5ae6-9af4-dfc7a684e8ee" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318537} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "284de721-bcb0-5584-98b1-7d0cec8bc940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "284de721-bcb0-5584-98b1-7d0cec8bc940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317690} :answer-field/id #uuid "9565b1c0-a7b6-54b9-9567-456ae91899dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "198c9f13-854c-5a78-a110-5897652614aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-346519" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$artanh\\,(${{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nNote that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C,\n$$\n\nthe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3/5}\\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{3/5}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{3/5} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})-4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f286994-9b4f-5dee-b8a9-1575e6938302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(2{e}^{3}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f286994-9b4f-5dee-b8a9-1575e6938302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(2{e}^{3}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318106} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a98dba4-54f8-5f32-8ce5-f4039b42cddc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4b4d889c-3125-509c-8bd0-37836a63b86f" :question/math-academy-id "q-346521" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 1\\cdot \\,dx=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}-{\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}v{u}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\,(x){|}_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}-{\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}x(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{3}}{4})\\,(\\frac{{e}^{3}}{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4})\\,(\\frac{1}{4})]-{\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3})-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)-x{|}_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2{e}^{3}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdf4bb63-02ff-5ebf-8e66-c6be8c85edd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\operatorname{ln}(2+\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdf4bb63-02ff-5ebf-8e66-c6be8c85edd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\operatorname{ln}(2+\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318195} :answer-field/id #uuid "64001866-4276-554f-984c-71378cda4ad0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6f5d1a83-72d6-5baf-8989-3f4b0012df34" :question/math-academy-id "q-346522" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}}\\,\\text{d}x=4{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡2-12\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡2 \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319118 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af226b09-d827-5c1c-aaf8-a979730d39f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af226b09-d827-5c1c-aaf8-a979730d39f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318157} :answer-field/id #uuid "f15b0103-0a30-53b1-b24e-a7de7389c80b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9846049f-0423-59a8-8569-7eab74f54e97" :question/math-academy-id "q-346555" :question/problem "Calculate $F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}4{sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n$F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4{sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4{sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =4(1-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319120 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0626dc1a-6232-5b90-9385-e5ce50b107d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15+\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0626dc1a-6232-5b90-9385-e5ce50b107d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15+\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317590} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb73beaa-867a-55f5-abae-114501789a36" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c755b337-b4d4-5ea7-9e4c-878004ac0d92" :question/math-academy-id "q-346571" :question/problem "Thermal energy is being added to a device at a rate of $3t$ joules per second. If at time $t=0$ the device already contains $15$ joules of energy, write an expression for $E(t),$ the total energy in the device at time $t,$ where $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\n$E(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(t)=3t$ represent the rate at which energy enters the device, and let $E(T)$ be the total energy in the device after $T$ seconds.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nE(T)=E(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(T) & =E(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =15+{\\int }_{0}^{T}3t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={15+(\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =15+\\frac{3{T}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nE(t)=15+\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319121 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd785ee6-6715-53f7-8cdf-ba030fe99857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd785ee6-6715-53f7-8cdf-ba030fe99857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318306} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fb330fb-3336-561a-98fa-cec7e2718d25" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2bb34ddd-c2ee-57af-a0a2-9fb98a895f33" :question/math-academy-id "q-346575" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}(1-{cot}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta $ with $\\theta =x-\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{cot}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & ={csc}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ {cot}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & ={csc}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}(1-{cot}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}(1-{csc}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}(2-{csc}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}2\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319122 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a96d98f9-09fc-51b5-a09c-4c2fde0fa6fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a96d98f9-09fc-51b5-a09c-4c2fde0fa6fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318141} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bb74c53-51d2-5629-8fd0-821887d13160" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b23fa132-091d-593b-a542-6eca17f50321" :question/math-academy-id "q-346576" :question/problem "The total number of pages of a document printed $t$ minutes after printing begins increases at a rate of $\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}.$ If, at $t=0$ minutes, the printer had already produced $40$ pages, how many pages will the printer have produced after $2$ minutes?\n\nAfter $2$ minutes the printer has produced\n{{field-1}} pages." :question/worked-solution "Let $a(t)=\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}$ be the rate at which the number of pages produced is changing, and let $A(T)$ be the number of pages produced $T$ minutes after printing begins. \n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =40+{\\int }_{0}^{T}\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =40+(-\\frac{18}{t+1}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =40+(-\\frac{18}{T+1})-(-\\frac{18}{1}) \\\\  & =40-\\frac{18}{T+1}+18 \\\\  & =58-\\frac{18}{T+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of pages produced $T=2$ minutes after printing begins, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nA(2)=40-\\frac{18}{2+1}+18=52.\n$$\n\nThus, after $2$ minutes, the printer has produced $52$ pages."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cc8ab50-9678-5fe2-a058-74e9262eb4f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cc8ab50-9678-5fe2-a058-74e9262eb4f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317660} :answer-field/id #uuid "b596f4a3-fa7b-57e3-b7e2-1fe01583da6e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6f3ba61f-934e-53ed-a21e-8c527c008d63" :question/math-academy-id "q-346702" :question/problem "![](images/78/7808295803d04d2c996ca7d36110f070a60f4491107ad1c831e6fa8ea9f85678.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\pi y) & =1 \\\\ \\pi y & =…,-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4},… \\\\ y & =…,-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{5}{4},…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that we want to find the area between the curve $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-\\frac{3}{4}$ to $y=\\frac{1}{4}.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3/4}^{1/4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3/4}^{1/4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-3/4}^{1/4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)}{\\pi }|}_{-3/4}^{1/4}-{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)}{\\pi }|}_{-3/4}^{1/4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})]-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})]-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is\n\n$$\nA=|-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }|=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da371f43-276c-5b18-9da5-af0ba94cea69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{2}{3})+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da371f43-276c-5b18-9da5-af0ba94cea69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{2}{3})+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318292} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d176162-d1f8-59b4-b435-50afc62c79e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f963953-4ca0-5b72-8632-5bdad8051d11" :question/math-academy-id "q-346710" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-4}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-4)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\nx+2=A(x-4)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+2 & =A(4-4)+B \\\\ B & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+2 & =A(0-4)+6 \\\\ 2 & =-4A+6 \\\\ 4A & =4 \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x-4}+\\frac{6}{(x-4)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x-4}+\\frac{6}{(x-4)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x+6{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|\\,{|}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{6}{x-4}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-4|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-4|)-(\\frac{6}{2-4}-\\frac{6}{1-4}) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-(-3-(-2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d279eda-667c-555e-8386-f505329564d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}3-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d279eda-667c-555e-8386-f505329564d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}3-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317986} :answer-field/id #uuid "30bd3f50-8128-5d2f-a7f6-94157ad1e225" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68d214ab-9577-5e8b-9200-4e23d1e48429" :question/math-academy-id "q-346752" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}-2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}-2x+1}=\\frac{x-4}{(x-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{B}{(x-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-1)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\nx-4=A(x-1)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-4 & =A(1-1)+B \\\\ B & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-4 & =A(0-1)+B \\\\ -4 & =-A-3 \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x-1}-\\frac{3}{(x-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{x-4}{(x-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}(\\frac{1}{x-1}-\\frac{3}{(x-1)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x-3{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|\\,{|}_{2}^{4}+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)+3(\\frac{1}{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{1})+3(-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25ee2626-1199-5d03-8d29-49c8714aadba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25ee2626-1199-5d03-8d29-49c8714aadba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317684} :answer-field/id #uuid "41efcb76-531e-5d4e-bbdc-2d53d1b9757d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318562} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a319b5d2-0ac1-592d-b731-73b0e1569f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a319b5d2-0ac1-592d-b731-73b0e1569f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318124} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ab4aa44-1646-5ef0-a44c-3eb1d7c25c9b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e232935-891f-5034-b933-fe05b7b0f382" :question/math-academy-id "q-346793" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{4x+23}{(5-2x)(x+3)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{4x+23}{(5-2x)(x+3)}=\\,$ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $5-2x$ |  | $x+3$ |" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x+23}{(5-2x)(x+3)}=\\frac{A}{5-2x}+\\frac{B}{x+3}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(5-2x)(x+3)$ gives\n\n$$\n4x+23=A(x+3)+B(5-2x).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-3)+23 & =A(-3+3)+B(5-2(-3)) \\\\ 11 & =11B \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(\\frac{5}{2})+23 & =A(\\frac{5}{2}+3)+B(5-2(\\frac{5}{2})) \\\\ 33 & =A(\\frac{11}{2}) \\\\ A & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x+23}{(5-2x)(x+3)}=\\frac{6}{5-2x}+\\frac{1}{x+3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3292633b-30ac-5c02-bb00-561e642ae309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3292633b-30ac-5c02-bb00-561e642ae309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317723} :answer-field/id #uuid "2861d4ee-99bc-55fd-980b-a15c95a09bea" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318506} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97177ef5-a170-5955-bfe0-424ef6f22683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97177ef5-a170-5955-bfe0-424ef6f22683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318081} :answer-field/id #uuid "41d65071-bc35-56ea-b328-5b09965b48e3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318561} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3fb85f9-4ad6-5774-bff4-0de9fba7fdd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3fb85f9-4ad6-5774-bff4-0de9fba7fdd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318129} :answer-field/id #uuid "c233b400-2ce4-5120-a868-2448b5aac6f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f2b918d3-6ba6-56f0-80d2-8d7b4daa7976" :question/math-academy-id "q-346796" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{5{x}^{2}+10x-21}{(x-1)(x+2)(2x-3)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{5{x}^{2}+10x-21}{(x-1)(x+2)(2x-3)}=\\,$ | {{field-1}} | $-$ | {{field-2}} | $+$ | {{field-3}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $x-1$ |  | $x+2$ |  | $2x-3$ |" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}+10x-21}{(x-1)(x+2)(2x-3)}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{B}{x+2}+\\frac{C}{2x-3}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x-1)(x+2)(2x-3)$ gives\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}+10x-21=A(x+2)(2x-3)+B(x-1)(2x-3)+C(x-1)(x+2).\n$$\n\nFor $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1)^{2}+10(1)-21 & =A(1+2)(2(1)-3)+B(1-1)(2(1)-3)+C(1-1)(1+2) \\\\ -6 & =-3A \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-2)^{2}+10(-2)-21 & =A(-2+2)(2(-2)-3)+B(-2-1)(2(-2)-3)+C(-2-1)(-2+2) \\\\ -21 & =21B \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+10(\\frac{3}{2})-21 & =A(\\frac{3}{2}+2)(2(\\frac{3}{2})-3)+B(\\frac{3}{2}-1)(2(\\frac{3}{2})-3)+C(\\frac{3}{2}-1)(\\frac{3}{2}+2) \\\\ \\frac{21}{4} & =\\frac{7}{4}C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}+10x-21}{(x-1)(x+2)(2x-3)}=\\frac{2}{x-1}-\\frac{1}{x+2}+\\frac{3}{2x-3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319145 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b433582c-2f7d-5011-a109-56570d612329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b433582c-2f7d-5011-a109-56570d612329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318174} :answer-field/id #uuid "0212c1b9-9229-5284-86c3-5ed77f20f012" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318427} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a8f9e1-4e87-56f5-963a-2d17aa1d8367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09a8f9e1-4e87-56f5-963a-2d17aa1d8367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317602} :answer-field/id #uuid "02faa55b-56ce-5fd7-a61f-3942784b6bf8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318430} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7917ed5-07be-504e-9ca9-f5cb015df1b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7917ed5-07be-504e-9ca9-f5cb015df1b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318385} :answer-field/id #uuid "20e3fef3-301d-5e4c-9f9c-1f104afbbca1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb591c3f-e5ec-5e76-968d-22d893bd432e" :question/math-academy-id "q-346871" :question/problem "What is the value of $f(t)=\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{2}+4}\\,i+{3}^{t}\\,j+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,k$ at $t=2?$\n\n$f(2)=$\n{{field-1}} $i\\,+\\,$\n\n{{field-2}} $j\\,+\\,$\n\n{{field-3}} $k$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate, we substitute $t=2$ into the function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =\\sqrt[3]{{2}^{2}+4}\\,i+{3}^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8}\\,i+{3}^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\pi )\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+9\\,j+0\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319149 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4433636-de54-5170-a857-396d891596aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4433636-de54-5170-a857-396d891596aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318131} :answer-field/id #uuid "31383c4f-cfa9-5007-b57d-2e5cd4edf56b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318525} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54c20fd1-e5d0-5ba9-99bf-7df4f0871ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54c20fd1-e5d0-5ba9-99bf-7df4f0871ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317848} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a98a53e-0ccc-5bb0-9b9b-c7afded015e5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318682} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ac6e641-ebff-5ba3-96ab-3a7269a51765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ac6e641-ebff-5ba3-96ab-3a7269a51765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317654} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b92fd65-9ef1-54b2-ab0f-14aec51ef8f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "807c4b87-85e6-5eed-9e1d-1e100a9b4aab" :question/math-academy-id "q-346957" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{5}{({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{5}{({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)}=\\,$ | {{field-1}}$\\,x\\,+$\n{{field-2}} | $+$ | {{field-3}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $8({x}^{2}+4)$ |  | $8(x+2)$ |" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A$, $B$, and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)}=\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{C}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)$ gives\n\n$$\n5=(Ax+B)(x+2)+C({x}^{2}+4).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =C(4+4) \\\\ 5 & =8C \\\\ C & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =(A(0)+B)(0+2)+\\frac{5}{8}((0)^{2}+4) \\\\ 5 & =2B+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 2B & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ B & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =(A(1)+\\frac{5}{4})(1+2)+\\frac{5}{8}((1)^{2}+4) \\\\ 5 & =3A+\\frac{15}{4}+\\frac{25}{8} \\\\ 3A & =-\\frac{15}{8} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{5}{8}x+\\frac{5}{4})}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{(\\frac{5}{8})}{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\,x+10}{8({x}^{2}+4)}+\\frac{5}{8(x+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e62c0ae-d8d1-54c4-b59e-37ff1225c3c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{40}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e62c0ae-d8d1-54c4-b59e-37ff1225c3c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{40}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317998} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2b15ad3-db26-57a4-ae32-bd15d4341f5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1fc7feda-9975-5f67-aaa7-81f6387a8c6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-347077" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)} & =\\frac{A}{(x-2)}+\\frac{B}{(x+4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2x+2=A(x+4)+B(x-2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-4)+2 & =A(-4+4)+B(-4-2) \\\\ -6 & =-6B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)+2 & =A(2+4)+B(2-2) \\\\ 6 & =6A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}=\\frac{1}{x-2}+\\frac{1}{x+4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-2}+\\frac{1}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-2)(x+4)|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-2)(x+4)|{|}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(40)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\  & =ln\\,(\\frac{40}{7}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f33917-c3bd-5b12-9b6b-0ce6f8b325a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}(t)+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01f33917-c3bd-5b12-9b6b-0ce6f8b325a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}(t)+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317576} :answer-field/id #uuid "a200f1e4-6bd5-5695-b6f2-1b9d16078323" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318782} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02080071-87ef-54d7-ace2-86ed19a7a892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(3t)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02080071-87ef-54d7-ace2-86ed19a7a892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(3t)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317578} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd5791a6-a1f2-5cc2-8148-a1c683f40b10" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da0cb5eb-230c-5c67-a533-8ac850a1bfba" :question/math-academy-id "q-347095" :question/problem "Find $F(t)$ given that \n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)\\,,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)⟩\n$$\n\n and $F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨4,\\,-2⟩.$\n\n$F(t)=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)\\,,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)\\,,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int -\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial condition $F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨4,\\,-2⟩$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨4,\\,-2⟩ \\\\ ⟨1+{c}_{1},\\,-1+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨4,\\,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${c}_{1}=3$ and ${c}_{2}=-1.$ Consequently\n\n$$\nF(t)=⟨\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)+3,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-1⟩.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319155 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b79c6239-a2b4-5adb-bff4-04a647c99f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b79c6239-a2b4-5adb-bff4-04a647c99f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318181} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1ff960c-dde6-51c4-9221-8694e66bf0d3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1effe8ba-c512-5938-b5b0-96467f99280a" :question/math-academy-id "q-347129" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{\\infty }\\frac{15}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{\\infty }\\frac{15}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =15\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{a}\\frac{1}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =15\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{a}\\frac{1}{(3x)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =15\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)]}_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{a} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})) \\\\  & =5\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/9f/9f882cb5102270dfe4bef256782c18aaeeca3518f564cd188298040237e30f62.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{\\infty }\\frac{15}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a7d255f-76ed-5d4d-9c15-25da71cd5728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a7d255f-76ed-5d4d-9c15-25da71cd5728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317871} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff050c97-a6d3-556d-b091-7af92711e18e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b7d4a2c-14e4-5bf2-b20c-66932dfe09d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-347189" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1),\\,\\frac{3}{t}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed $|v|$ of the particle at time $t=3.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe speed at time $t=3$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t+1},\\,-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=3,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨\\frac{1}{3+1},\\,-\\frac{3}{{3}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=3$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(3),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(3)| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{144}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319173 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adbb49ea-0d78-52cf-ba8f-13798c4ec7b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adbb49ea-0d78-52cf-ba8f-13798c4ec7b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318154} :answer-field/id #uuid "1abfac5e-bbd1-5a4f-a845-423b3e81013e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318481} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b514cd4-62f9-597b-89cf-e6b5db7905e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b514cd4-62f9-597b-89cf-e6b5db7905e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317657} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cc67bbe-7474-5dc8-b7db-9eaf9678451a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "226f50bc-b843-5766-9cf3-7f8c6585337d" :question/math-academy-id "q-347229" :question/problem "Find the range of values of $k$ for which the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{9}^{kn}}{{3}^{8n}}$\nconverges. Do not round the answer.\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{9}^{kn}}{{3}^{8n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{9}^{k}}{{3}^{8}})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{9}^{k}}{{9}^{4}})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{({9}^{k-4})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r={9}^{k-4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{9}^{k-4}| & <1 \\\\ {9}^{k-4} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because ${9}^{k-4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${9}^{k-4}=1$ has the solution $k=4.$ Now, since ${9}^{k-4}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${9}^{k-4}<1$ must be $k<4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "797bc2a6-2341-55dd-873d-4b6ac0c84ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "797bc2a6-2341-55dd-873d-4b6ac0c84ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317977} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f8ca8af-f6ee-533e-b29c-ab3346bd7b7f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318518} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2ab4558-3a85-5515-bef0-c07596fb4c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2ab4558-3a85-5515-bef0-c07596fb4c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318169} :answer-field/id #uuid "974cc391-37e1-57a4-94d9-469fa48eb9be" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318759} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3046fc10-9d94-5a65-9f58-734286a4697c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3046fc10-9d94-5a65-9f58-734286a4697c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317711} :answer-field/id #uuid "c351f8a2-9df9-5565-bda3-25f3df12e50e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a788bb34-e61a-56de-b053-7c383ccbe7d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-347485" :question/problem "Find the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}(x)$ of $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n${P}_{2}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,+$\n{{field-3}}${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we plug $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ and ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡(x)} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{cos}^{3}⁡(x)} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ and ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}+3(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+3(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+3{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319184 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ab5ee3f-1c0c-55aa-a987-3e47b79d749c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ab5ee3f-1c0c-55aa-a987-3e47b79d749c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317934} :answer-field/id #uuid "866942c4-c6fb-53e8-9a41-30c0a30ba46a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318716} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f8aa453-c5a3-5258-a02c-823abe0262ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f8aa453-c5a3-5258-a02c-823abe0262ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317770} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7fc80ba-c574-50e8-872c-fb745605e2d9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318912} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e60bc73c-1331-5696-a1c2-d3297d81e49e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e60bc73c-1331-5696-a1c2-d3297d81e49e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318334} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8a7c333-1ff0-5461-9df5-9b27ba61db41" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6392f233-064f-5acb-81b9-e3a0f8e2d3bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-347514" :question/problem "Find the interval of convergence for the following series:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}=\\frac{x}{4\\cdot 3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}\\cdot 8}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{4}^{3}\\cdot 13}+⋯\n$$\n\nThe interval of convergence is\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{4}^{n+1}(5n+3)}\\cdot \\frac{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x(5n-2)}{4(5n+3)}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5n-2}{5n+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{|x|}{4}<1\\,⟹\\,|x|<4.\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $4,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-4,4).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=4,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-4$ and $x=4$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{5n-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{13}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see if this series converges, we use the limit comparison test, with ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{5n-2}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n}.$ We observe that ${a}_{n}$ and ${b}_{n}$ are positive, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{5n-2})}{(\\frac{1}{n})}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{5n-2}=\\frac{1}{5},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is positive and finite. Hence, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ either *both* converge or *both* diverge.\nSince ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n}$ is divergent, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{5n-2}$ is also divergent by the limit comparison test. So, the endpoint $x=4$ is not included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-4)^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{5n-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{13}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test. So, the endpoint $x=-4$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-4,4),$ or equivalently, $-4\\le x<4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319185 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b2a878a-69e0-5281-8468-b64406ed262d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b2a878a-69e0-5281-8468-b64406ed262d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317814} :answer-field/id #uuid "2019e334-f3f9-55a3-b58a-3ed19a8b58f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ed4d6fb-9e42-5a64-ad35-6dec89e5b2ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-347518" :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{n}^{3}(x-2)^{n}=(x-2)+{2}^{3}(x-2)^{2}+{3}^{3}(x-2)^{3}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}={n}^{3}(x-2)^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{3}(x-2)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}(x-2)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{3}(x-2)}{{n}^{3}}| \\\\  & =|x-2|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n+1}{n})}^{3} \\\\  & =|x-2|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x-2|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x-2|<1.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1670a3cf-9743-54e9-8189-836f7fa55225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 23 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317635} {:answer/id #uuid "3666f00b-c376-5f08-a75e-4782964f8dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -\\frac{37}{3} \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317740} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7e0096-3b52-5db5-b3ac-0b6ebf5f8c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 26 \\\\ 27\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317872} {:answer/id #uuid "83730cd0-d5e3-5d0a-a49e-ed307de58909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318007} {:answer/id #uuid "f864fa30-dd03-571e-9a9a-36c8567c5ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -34 \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f864fa30-dd03-571e-9a9a-36c8567c5ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -34 \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318388} :answer-field/id #uuid "56f47f49-1ef5-57cb-904a-c3e3077963fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26071956-0b9c-584b-9262-4d72b8945fc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-34760" :question/problem "Find the vector $x$, if $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ and \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that any vector in  $\\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,B.$ Since we are told that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\underbrace{(\\frac{3}{2})}_{{x}_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}+\\underbrace{(-3)}_{{x}_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ -30 \\\\ -54\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -34 \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3321115a-1180-5c5b-a989-d9b89f611d25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3321115a-1180-5c5b-a989-d9b89f611d25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317727} :answer-field/id #uuid "85aee846-359f-5b08-a9de-8f4adcb72510" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318714} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dec84e90-2e10-5b17-9206-5ad94172a7d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dec84e90-2e10-5b17-9206-5ad94172a7d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318309} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4350e71-8c5c-57c1-9d01-7580966bb9fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318813} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0fe7eca-c66a-5883-af97-4d540b7c6068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0fe7eca-c66a-5883-af97-4d540b7c6068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318256} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4de9581-0ac4-5575-8ce8-8bdb9a196a90" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "756a953d-5a94-5865-8d60-c69c435be895" :question/math-academy-id "q-347614" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ about the point $x=2.$\n\n$\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=${{field-1}}\n$-\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-2)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-2)^{2}-⋯$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and $a=2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}={x}^{-2} \\\\ f(2) & ={2}^{-2}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}=-2{x}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-2(2)^{-3}=-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}=6{x}^{-4} \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =6(2)^{-4}=\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{4}+(-\\frac{1}{4})(x-2)+\\frac{(\\frac{3}{8})}{2!}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{4})}{3!}(x-2)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}(x-2)+\\frac{3}{16}(x-2)^{2}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319189 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30cd0d9b-5fe9-51b3-9519-02b039db9514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30cd0d9b-5fe9-51b3-9519-02b039db9514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317715} :answer-field/id #uuid "89037d38-162e-5bd4-86ef-575cf06fd386" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "347dab61-bb37-50f6-ab92-42466a2565f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-347615" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{(x/5)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\cdot 5{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{5})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}+\\frac{3\\pi }{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319192 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a003a1d1-8ee3-58b9-861c-8527f9522a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a003a1d1-8ee3-58b9-861c-8527f9522a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318112} :answer-field/id #uuid "2837f48b-1a7c-56c4-a073-cfae4a30010a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fcea307-0381-5580-bd2c-78e4cb1e7349" :question/math-academy-id "q-347621" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=5+{x}^{8}$,  then $\\text{d}u=8{x}^{7}\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5+{x}^{8}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{5+{x}^{8}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5+0}-\\frac{1}{5+{a}^{8}}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{5+{a}^{8}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "897332b3-8c34-50c6-9aee-61a82f3ed1e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{219}{200}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "897332b3-8c34-50c6-9aee-61a82f3ed1e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{219}{200}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318028} :answer-field/id #uuid "be66636a-8532-51b6-aa7b-719f82340b31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d0330bc-2c1d-5dd3-9a79-e9c8a3180d87" :question/math-academy-id "q-347635" :question/problem "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=1$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{1.2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\sqrt{1.2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $a=1.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(1) & ={1}^{1/2}=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)+\\frac{(-1/4)}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)-\\frac{1}{8}(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1.2$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{1.2}.$ Since\n\n$$\n1.2-1=\\frac{1}{5},\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1.2} & ≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{5})-\\frac{1}{8}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{10}-\\frac{1}{200} \\\\  & =\\frac{219}{200}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07472bd2-a9cd-522a-9f52-0b681eccc11e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{51}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07472bd2-a9cd-522a-9f52-0b681eccc11e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{51}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317595} :answer-field/id #uuid "3958f52b-2459-596b-beba-67dac94e6558" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2c3ff0dd-0084-5332-a5b5-9ae0db80dd2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-347637" :question/problem "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ about $x=\\pi $ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi +0.2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi +0.2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and $a=\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi )=-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\pi )=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{tan}^{2}⁡x+{sec}^{3}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi ){tan}^{2}⁡(\\pi )+{sec}^{3}⁡(\\pi )=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-1+(0)(x-\\pi )+\\frac{(-1)}{2!}(x-\\pi )^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\pi +0.2$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi +0.2).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi +0.2) & ≈-1-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\pi +0.2-\\pi )}^{2} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)^{2} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{25}) \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{50} \\\\  & =-\\frac{51}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19cfa2e0-d54c-572f-b8bd-4f33e0950b64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19cfa2e0-d54c-572f-b8bd-4f33e0950b64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317650} :answer-field/id #uuid "31ca60d2-e433-59b2-8aa9-7176db6fedfe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318526} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cee93a28-42f7-5a58-94e0-48f329383dc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cee93a28-42f7-5a58-94e0-48f329383dc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318249} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb3aa437-3a1f-5460-9133-7d610bb54bed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a87c6bb4-7854-5fc3-a9aa-5ab23d8f133c" :question/math-academy-id "q-347770" :question/problem "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n+1}}{{x}^{n}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ Find the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges.\n\n$x$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{5}^{n+1}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot {5}^{n}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{n}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =5{(\\frac{5}{x})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{5}{x}.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{5}{x}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,x\\ge 5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57db562f-825a-5c06-9c18-a4185051c072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57db562f-825a-5c06-9c18-a4185051c072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317861} :answer-field/id #uuid "5672edce-69fb-5725-aa5f-85f880ba3571" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95cfa877-83ba-5877-86e4-7c000e0d1f2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-347839" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3y\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x),\\,y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1.\n$$\n\nYou are given that $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We are given that $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1$ into the solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\ 1 & =Asin\\,(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ 1 & =Asin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ 1 & =A(1) \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc63ae4e-cfaa-53ce-820b-6b955f4e241e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi /8}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc63ae4e-cfaa-53ce-820b-6b955f4e241e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi /8}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318407} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c3bf0c6-3804-5ad7-986b-8aafdaaff36d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc5a81b5-b9fd-57fc-867d-023dfe7eb508" :question/math-academy-id "q-347926" :question/problem "![](images/2d/2da83e2ec620d3920c4378a77785a9ef2802dd1d06badcf4852be2263f68264e.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r=6{e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above). Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=6\\sqrt{2}($\n{{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=6{e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=6{e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{(6{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{(6{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{36{e}^{2\\theta }+36{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{72{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{e}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /8} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /8}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /8}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "413af386-018a-5a79-9059-adefc2791bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "413af386-018a-5a79-9059-adefc2791bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317776} :answer-field/id #uuid "487a3ee4-df99-519f-a750-20dbe1bce54e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8e9f1bb-dfe9-519e-8bb3-e8a484b04ce5" :question/math-academy-id "q-348086" :question/problem "![](images/12/121012fc5671ada263e83ec6d3a897d187ecb3a9d56c8e4331a3c0c608a92622.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63e7b79b-6298-5b7e-aa80-09f258e9f2ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63e7b79b-6298-5b7e-aa80-09f258e9f2ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317907} :answer-field/id #uuid "1499962a-f058-50a8-a54d-83b434d7fef1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c09908c-2ee7-5200-b887-daef1f475ca5" :question/math-academy-id "q-348088" :question/problem "![](images/be/be9e314ed11882842bbf9a74deeb3a994596c8275a4e854ffc66327fa0008058.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{{(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\pi )^{2}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =2{\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96314937-c536-5bae-9e39-cf844b51de53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96314937-c536-5bae-9e39-cf844b51de53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318078} :answer-field/id #uuid "52e1c87e-231d-557c-9bd5-7ae92841d688" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0877a5c5-e4d1-511a-acaa-d1f39d42fd10" :question/math-academy-id "q-348093" :question/problem "For the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ),$ evaluate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,\\pi /4}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the numerator and denominator at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+(-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-(-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,{|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,\\pi /4} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "639260c0-53c6-59b3-b1e6-e1986d9bc6a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "639260c0-53c6-59b3-b1e6-e1986d9bc6a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317904} :answer-field/id #uuid "5974d30a-ec36-5215-95ba-60fa8dcde0ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318618} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85532a8a-4f40-5913-9cd2-e0f803e535aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85532a8a-4f40-5913-9cd2-e0f803e535aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318019} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a671c7d-1105-5123-bb57-8d5976a419d3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318680} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e39ced00-b89f-5c02-9cc9-48217c75aed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e39ced00-b89f-5c02-9cc9-48217c75aed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318321} :answer-field/id #uuid "921ca22d-21df-5fb0-b1b9-8be6064c63ba" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ad040194-971d-54b7-8f33-bceb77563250" :question/math-academy-id "q-348121" :question/problem "Find the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}(x)$ of  ${sin}^{2}⁡x$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${P}_{3}(x)=\\frac{3}{4}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,-$\n{{field-2}}${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{2}\\,-$\n\n{{field-3}}${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the third-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ we plug $a=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\frac{1}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{2}+{f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\frac{1}{3!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{3} \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to compute $f(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ and ${f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={sin}^{2}⁡x & ⟹ &  & f(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & ={sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})={(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=2(-\\frac{1}{2})=-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & ⟹ &  & {f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=-4(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug the results for $f(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ and ${f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ into our third-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{(-1)}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{2}+\\frac{(-2\\sqrt{3})}{6}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c452c45-e9e5-5b9c-a9dc-12ef821fd6bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}{sec}^{3}(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c452c45-e9e5-5b9c-a9dc-12ef821fd6bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}{sec}^{3}(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317610} :answer-field/id #uuid "051bb36f-1d11-5b10-b2ec-04d162731ca0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcdb0d99-88c2-5541-9046-61f9f5e56a06" :question/math-academy-id "q-348139" :question/problem "For the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ calculate $\\frac{{d}^{2}y}{d{x}^{2}}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n$\\frac{{d}^{2}y}{d{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{d}^{2}y}{d{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ and $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }.$ \n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta .$ \n\nUsing the fact that $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,y=3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nThen, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ))}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea0640d9-c3e5-5ddf-9df9-feeb259286d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea0640d9-c3e5-5ddf-9df9-feeb259286d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318345} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b191dcb-cda9-53a5-951a-047fd95e08bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03e05686-97cf-5c15-b403-a4caf1953954" :question/math-academy-id "q-348275" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{1/3}\\frac{7}{4}x\\sqrt[3]{6x+6}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=6x+6.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x=\\frac{1}{6}(u-6).$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $-1$ | $1/3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $0$ | $8$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1/3}\\frac{7}{4}x\\sqrt[3]{6x+6}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}(u-6)\\cdot {u}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{144}{\\int }_{0}^{8}({u}^{4/3}-6{u}^{1/3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{144}{(\\frac{1}{(7/3)}{u}^{7/3}-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{(4/3)}{u}^{4/3})|}_{0}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{144}{(\\frac{3}{7}{u}^{7/3}-\\frac{9}{2}{u}^{4/3})|}_{0}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{144}[(\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot {8}^{7/3}-\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot {8}^{4/3})-(\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot {0}^{7/3}-\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot {0}^{4/3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{144}(\\frac{384}{7}-72) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{144}\\cdot (-\\frac{120}{7}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "730ade57-923a-5b77-b9d1-e09ab5c9256d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "730ade57-923a-5b77-b9d1-e09ab5c9256d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317960} :answer-field/id #uuid "acfb67b2-49ba-5e80-bd44-691f651b8f48" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4037d146-183a-5606-a4b5-d0636799ff9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-348282" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{48(x+2)}{{({x}^{2}+4x)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={x}^{2}+4x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x+4\\,⟹\\,2(x+2)\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $-3$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $-3$ | $-4$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{48(x+2)}{{({x}^{2}+4x)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =24{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{1}{{({x}^{2}+4x)}^{3}}\\cdot 2(x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =24{\\int }_{-3}^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =24{\\int }_{-3}^{-4}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =24\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-2})|}_{-3}^{-4} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{12}{{u}^{2}})|}_{-3}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{(-4)^{2}}-(-\\frac{12}{(-3)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{16}-(-\\frac{12}{9}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f92673c-93e1-5be6-93ae-6ffaadf6d7db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f92673c-93e1-5be6-93ae-6ffaadf6d7db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318055} :answer-field/id #uuid "d48ddeb2-9295-55b0-9f0d-6c6f6b11577f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eae00a34-daa5-5a67-90d9-6bc0c0ecdb5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-348283" :question/problem "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ At time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ the particle is located at the position $x=1.$ \nFind the position of the particle at time $t=\\pi .$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is equal to its position at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\pi ].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(\\pi )=x(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\pi ) & =1+{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =1+{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})]}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =1+([-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4})]-[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})]) \\\\  & =1+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =1+(-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+1) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1 \\\\  & =2+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is $x=2+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73575143-4460-538c-8aa1-056786aecc43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73575143-4460-538c-8aa1-056786aecc43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317962} :answer-field/id #uuid "8176f5f0-47d6-5f0f-ba47-63f839d87ff0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a0c50af-6c83-593f-8570-9f596da85472" :question/math-academy-id "q-348295" :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x={t}^{2}+1,\\,y={t}^{3}+4.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}t,$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,2}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3}{2}t,$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{2}+1)=2t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{3}{2}t)}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,2}=\\frac{3}{4(2)}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94afa6f7-9ec3-5674-aaf4-34a2e1a3c792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{192\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94afa6f7-9ec3-5674-aaf4-34a2e1a3c792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{192\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318073} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e9a7e53-8eec-5495-85b5-fb5492802a0f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "88eff08e-1bbb-529f-965a-96596e4595fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-348298" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=6t-2{t}^{3},\\,y=6{t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-2{t}^{3})=6-6{t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2})=12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(6-6{t}^{2})}^{2}+{(12t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36-72{t}^{2}+36{t}^{4}+144{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36{t}^{4}+72{t}^{2}+36}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression $36{t}^{4}+72{t}^{2}+36,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36({t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\cdot 6\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\cdot |{t}^{2}+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =72\\pi [(\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3})-(\\frac{0}{5}+\\frac{0}{3})] \\\\  & =72\\pi \\cdot \\frac{8}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{192\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70131d8e-e110-5e84-bcff-45651751b52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70131d8e-e110-5e84-bcff-45651751b52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317953} :answer-field/id #uuid "805f7f24-9fb6-5e8f-8459-868c76a93452" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab4d806a-76c8-563a-a10d-c7fabb39c7b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-348304" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7ae65c873838ede4cce89c953ff2654fa1e58e65fdc87896c8ba85bbf947be20.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /4} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76c17acb-6457-5886-9ff2-ff91bb4ecbbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76c17acb-6457-5886-9ff2-ff91bb4ecbbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317973} :answer-field/id #uuid "874e2d16-8e34-579f-8e23-5bc989a9da8d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "084c0a1a-3b23-52a0-be86-625685b44c64" :question/math-academy-id "q-348330" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{-1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-x(x+2)}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x(x+2) & =-({x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x+[{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}]) \\\\  & =-((x+1)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(x+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{-1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-x(x+2)}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-1}^{-1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+1)$.\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u$.\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $-1/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1/2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{-1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-x(x+2)}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\arcsin\\,(\\frac{1}{2})-\\arcsin\\,(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "304ba8a2-d6e2-5916-8283-099a63b493ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{4}{5})+\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "304ba8a2-d6e2-5916-8283-099a63b493ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{4}{5})+\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317712} :answer-field/id #uuid "949120a2-97b4-5bb0-a7f5-ce16e986ff6b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1d29f74-3ab5-58b3-9701-b4401085ff7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-348350" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x+3}{(x-5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{(x-5)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-5}+\\frac{B}{(x-5)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-5)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\nx+3=A(x-5)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=5,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5+3 & =A(5-5)+B \\\\ B & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+3 & =A(0-5)+8 \\\\ 3 & =-5A+8 \\\\ 5A & =5 \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{(x-5)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x-5}+\\frac{8}{(x-5)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x+3}{(x-5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{x-5}+\\frac{8}{(x-5)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-5}\\,\\text{d}x+8{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-5|\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-\\frac{8}{x-5}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-5|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|0-5|)-(\\frac{8}{1-5}-\\frac{8}{0-5}) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)-(-2-(-\\frac{8}{5})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{5})+\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319253 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caa5522d-84dd-5929-b7e4-41606f76d20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{6}{5})-\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "caa5522d-84dd-5929-b7e4-41606f76d20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{6}{5})-\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318236} :answer-field/id #uuid "645da014-7ccd-5611-b0a7-bd19b901c215" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23a998c2-d0ee-57a9-9649-0245890af7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-348426" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x+1}{(x+5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{(x+5)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x+5}+\\frac{B}{(x+5)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+5)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\nx+1=A(x+5)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-5,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5+1 & =A(-5+5)+B \\\\ B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+1 & =A(0+5)-4 \\\\ 1 & =5A-4 \\\\ 5A & =5 \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{(x+5)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x+5}-\\frac{4}{(x+5)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x+1}{(x+5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{x+5}-\\frac{4}{(x+5)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x+5}\\,\\text{d}x-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+5|\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{4}{x+5}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+5|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|0+5|)+(\\frac{4}{1+5}-\\frac{4}{0+5}) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)+(\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{5})-\\frac{2}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65e9975c-1bdc-5628-a25b-036ad5ef4f35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}3}{2}+\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65e9975c-1bdc-5628-a25b-036ad5ef4f35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}3}{2}+\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317914} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac5ecd49-16d3-5305-8c3e-09eec361ecd8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9f15bc0-efc2-57a6-80e1-e0ac9f6a8276" :question/math-academy-id "q-348473" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{8-x}{x(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8-x}{x(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x-4}+\\frac{C}{(x-4)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x(x-4)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n8-x=A(x-4)^{2}+Bx(x-4)+Cx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and $C$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-0 & =A(0-4)^{2}+B(0)(0-4)+C(0) \\\\ 8 & =16A \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-4 & =A(4-4)^{2}+B(4)(4-4)+C(4) \\\\ 4 & =4C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known values of $A$ and $C,$ we can solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-1 & =A(1-4)^{2}+B(1)(1-4)+C(1) \\\\ 7 & =9A-3B+C \\\\ 7 & =9(\\frac{1}{2})-3B+1 \\\\ B & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{8-x}{x(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2(x-4)}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{8-x}{x(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2(x-4)}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|\\,{|}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|\\,{|}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x-4}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}+\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319255 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd5ee984-6d80-5e81-b647-b2efb1b7c50a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}4-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd5ee984-6d80-5e81-b647-b2efb1b7c50a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}4-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318411} :answer-field/id #uuid "88591a36-5e22-59fa-9309-9ac0822bfa60" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ae593e0-851a-5fb0-b188-6070b7e5f252" :question/math-academy-id "q-348521" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{2-x}{{x}^{2}-12x+36}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2-x}{{x}^{2}-12x+36}=\\frac{2-x}{(x-6)^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2-x}{(x-6)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-6}+\\frac{B}{(x-6)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-6)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n2-x=A(x-6)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=6,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-6 & =A(6-6)+B \\\\ B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-0 & =A(0-6)-4 \\\\ 2 & =-6A-4 \\\\ A & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2-x}{(x-6)^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{x-6}-\\frac{4}{(x-6)^{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{2-x}{(x-6)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}(-\\frac{1}{x-6}-\\frac{4}{(x-6)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{x-6}\\,\\text{d}x-4{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{(x-6)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|\\,{|}_{2}^{5}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-6}\\,{|}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)+4(-1-(-\\frac{1}{4})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+4(-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319257 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "457b53c0-5c6d-516d-a877-c712b70b39e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "457b53c0-5c6d-516d-a877-c712b70b39e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317791} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3d3deba-cbdd-5184-8055-2e4be419e417" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "935ea227-3967-5286-b590-f5c1cff1d8bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-348530" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-2+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{0}\\frac{3}{|x+2|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x+3 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+({2}^{2}-{2}^{2})+3 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2})-4+3 \\\\  & =(x+2)^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{0}\\frac{3}{|x+2|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x=3{\\int }_{-2+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{0}\\frac{1}{|x+2|\\sqrt{(x+2)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+2).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-2+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{0}\\frac{3}{|x+2|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\,\\text{arcsec}\\,u\\,{|}_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2} \\\\  & =3(\\text{arcsec}(2)-\\text{arcsec}\\,(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3df062f-a483-5b34-b334-45ecc7d4e171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3df062f-a483-5b34-b334-45ecc7d4e171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318270} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9736f4f-99a2-5e67-8623-ab108ae29d34" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7023621-9b53-549a-bf91-15f518a7082a" :question/math-academy-id "q-348533" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-4+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{-2}\\frac{5}{|x+4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+8x+15 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+({4}^{2}-{4}^{2})+15 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2})-16+15 \\\\  & =(x+4)^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{-2}\\frac{5}{|x+4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x=5{\\int }_{-4+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{-2}\\frac{1}{|x+4|\\sqrt{(x+4)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+4).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-4+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{-2}\\frac{5}{|x+4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{arcsec}\\,u\\,{|}_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2} \\\\  & =5(\\text{arcsec}(2)-\\text{arcsec}\\,(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\  & =5(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "354ccba6-4642-55d3-971d-ff4ca55e1306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "354ccba6-4642-55d3-971d-ff4ca55e1306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317734} :answer-field/id #uuid "4372872a-43e8-5e7f-bcf2-e151efaff712" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bda87ebf-c11d-539d-beb7-42e0b943e143" :question/math-academy-id "q-348547" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{-1+\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-2x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-2x & =-({x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x+[{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}]) \\\\  & =-((x+1)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(x+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{-1+\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-2x}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-1}^{-1+\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+1)$.\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u$.\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $-1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{-1+\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2} \\\\  & =\\arcsin\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319262 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5234ffcf-f5a8-57ff-9b16-9b9c2f10aee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5234ffcf-f5a8-57ff-9b16-9b9c2f10aee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317837} :answer-field/id #uuid "67416e31-0417-50b7-b78f-028611abdfb1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85ef0dc2-d9d3-5cf0-9efe-27fab192419c" :question/math-academy-id "q-348550" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-10}^{-8}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-18x-80}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-18x-80 & =-({x}^{2}+18x+80) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 9x+[{9}^{2}-{9}^{2}]+80) \\\\  & =-((x+9)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(x+9)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-10}^{-8}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-18x-80}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-10}^{-8}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+9)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+9).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+9.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-10$ | $-8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-10}^{-8}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-18x-80}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319264 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "778a582d-ea0f-55e8-bf04-1f6f33d24848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "778a582d-ea0f-55e8-bf04-1f6f33d24848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317974} :answer-field/id #uuid "64e67d9c-4cdd-54b3-9e59-0a2243fd10d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ae8ff40-323b-5106-b233-450ffba3e186" :question/math-academy-id "q-348554" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+5 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+({2}^{2}-{2}^{2})+5 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2})-4+5 \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{6}{(x-2)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x-2).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x-2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =6{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =6(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c473cf-9a6d-5243-afee-2a9a22ea295c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24c473cf-9a6d-5243-afee-2a9a22ea295c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317680} :answer-field/id #uuid "02cb7d9f-15cb-5de3-b16f-f7c775c110ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2760d8ee-c80d-54ee-9960-fe327a71f143" :question/math-academy-id "q-348563" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-7}^{\\sqrt{3}-6}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+12x+37}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+12x+37 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+({6}^{2}-{6}^{2})+37 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2})-36+37 \\\\  & =(x+6)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+12x+37}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{6}{(x+6)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x+6)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+6).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-7$ | $\\sqrt{3}-6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-7}^{\\sqrt{3}-6}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+12x+37}\\,\\text{d}x & =6{\\int }_{-1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\,{|}_{-1}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =6(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319267 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76643950-59ab-5f7a-be34-ee1ed0632a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76643950-59ab-5f7a-be34-ee1ed0632a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317971} :answer-field/id #uuid "963661af-e3ad-557e-a178-857097572d61" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65ee6f5f-82eb-5042-a628-9182bdef4e50" :question/math-academy-id "q-348570" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{4-1/\\sqrt{3}}^{5}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-8x+17}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x+17 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+({4}^{2}-{4}^{2})+17 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2})-16+17 \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-8x+17}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{(x-4)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x-4)$.\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x-4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u$.\n\n| $x$ | $4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u$, and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4-1/\\sqrt{3}}^{5}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-8x+17}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{-1/\\sqrt{3}}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\,{|}_{-1/\\sqrt{3}}^{1} \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\arctan\\,(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319270 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d20c5f42-22da-52e8-b97e-8c3cadcfc5bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{ln}2-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d20c5f42-22da-52e8-b97e-8c3cadcfc5bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{ln}2-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318261} :answer-field/id #uuid "57b19a01-237d-56f4-9a30-0aae241f29c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c31ac57-df2f-5a06-bb1c-13aa8aef1321" :question/math-academy-id "q-348617" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{-5}\\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}+8x+16}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}+8x+16}=\\frac{3x+7}{(x+4)^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+7}{(x+4)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x+4}+\\frac{B}{(x+4)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+4)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n3x+7=A(x+4)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-4)+7 & =A(-4+4)+B \\\\ B & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)+7 & =A(0+4)-5 \\\\ 7 & =4A-5 \\\\ A & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+7}{(x+4)^{2}}=\\frac{3}{x+4}-\\frac{5}{(x+4)^{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{-5}\\frac{3x+7}{(x+4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-6}^{-5}(\\frac{3}{x+4}-\\frac{5}{(x+4)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-6}^{-5}\\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x-5{\\int }_{-6}^{-5}\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|\\,{|}_{-6}^{-5}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,{|}_{-6}^{-5} \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)+5(-1-(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})+5(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319271 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8c23d41-f00a-5783-9c22-73f6f7125dfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8c23d41-f00a-5783-9c22-73f6f7125dfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318391} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e604446-60da-5bf5-a008-4345a74181f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16ed9bc1-6ff2-5ea8-83a6-05213b0f9561" :question/math-academy-id "q-348618" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{5}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5{ln}^{4}⁡x}{x}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{5}⁡x)(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4})(\\frac{5{ln}^{4}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{4}-5\\int \\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{4}-5I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{4}-5I \\\\ 6I & =\\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{4} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{24}({ln}^{6}⁡x)\\,{|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}({ln}^{6}⁡({e}^{3})-{ln}^{6}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}((3\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{6}-{0}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}({3}^{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{243}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01fa5805-5e53-540f-8ef9-b8839647b9c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01fa5805-5e53-540f-8ef9-b8839647b9c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317577} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c8a6544-5405-5835-a4fd-5921ce002c83" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318622} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51a0351c-fc5b-58c2-a35b-f41752e2dc8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51a0351c-fc5b-58c2-a35b-f41752e2dc8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317835} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ab37bf7-28ea-58ef-94c3-e0ebed66c0ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318683} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a09b0696-4358-5168-8046-92654a0ccd43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a09b0696-4358-5168-8046-92654a0ccd43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318113} :answer-field/id #uuid "e37b2db3-c45f-5535-ae82-3c77e80f9c02" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b75c4b0-8999-511a-bf30-9006271eb05a" :question/math-academy-id "q-348649" :question/problem "What is the value of $h(t)=({t}^{3}-1)\\,i+{e}^{\\,t-1}\\,j+\\sqrt[4]{t+15}\\,sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,k$ at $t=1?$\n\n$h(1)=$\n{{field-1}} $i\\,+\\,$\n\n{{field-2}} $j\\,+\\,$\n\n{{field-3}} $k$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate, we substitute $t=1$ into the function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(1) & =({1}^{3}-1)\\,i+{e}^{\\,1-1}\\,j+\\sqrt[4]{1+15}\\,sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i+{e}^{0}\\,j+\\sqrt[4]{16}\\cdot 1\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i+1\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99ccd0c5-c9f5-5a83-9bcb-a6eedad3e8a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99ccd0c5-c9f5-5a83-9bcb-a6eedad3e8a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318092} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8fba009-9b2f-5de7-983c-3162568cd196" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "467bbe03-18b4-513c-ba49-7eedf1cc8a92" :question/math-academy-id "q-348690" :question/problem "Given that $r(t)=⟨cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}),\\,\\sqrt{{t}^{3}-4},\\,ln\\,(2\\,{e}^{t-2}-1)⟩,$ what is $|r(2)|?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$|r(2)|=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating the function at $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}),\\,\\sqrt{{2}^{3}-4},\\,ln\\,(2\\,{e}^{0}-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ),\\,\\sqrt{4},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,2,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r(2)| & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b75d8886-10ce-5410-99ed-40552cc23aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b75d8886-10ce-5410-99ed-40552cc23aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318180} :answer-field/id #uuid "68a320fc-4e2c-5167-96e2-467a06e50b2d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c835cb3-d34e-5a3e-93e9-faa531eb53cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-348705" :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae150f223d210225b6490bebb859909b07842a8b7abb41137cf0206312aead2e.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}),$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\pi ,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319283 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82c2dc0c-77fe-5ca8-a2d0-814dd9e1e9e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82c2dc0c-77fe-5ca8-a2d0-814dd9e1e9e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318005} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae06ddb7-c5f0-5086-9930-28b12e1abea8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0693a2a7-f309-5977-8582-ff59fce11d62" :question/math-academy-id "q-348715" :question/problem "![](images/41/41304b7f8cbaf3df778e0f079a1651d2846f94dc74012db3b2ce936144eac8c7.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}),$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11cf6be8-1007-539b-810f-86366092fbb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11cf6be8-1007-539b-810f-86366092fbb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317622} :answer-field/id #uuid "35422f7b-79b8-5fda-8963-b8df2423b5de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5161378e-fb37-51cc-9749-945f16c22d40" :question/math-academy-id "q-348716" :question/problem "![](images/a2/a2ccde221d71e611f7917d6dac0d12ca6b8204d9cd80a21d79005195245cf77a.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{6}),$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,{|}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58f9ef55-4324-51a4-b956-83cd44d9bb99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58f9ef55-4324-51a4-b956-83cd44d9bb99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317865} :answer-field/id #uuid "14f3200e-6fd9-5fcf-97c5-28f1460aa4b6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4980ddb6-b73d-592c-82d0-e2e9983cdd14" :question/math-academy-id "q-348719" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}({cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2})-{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{3x}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)={cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2})-{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}({cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2})-{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319286 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6904022f-0eb5-5d2b-ab8e-9ea0929edd9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6904022f-0eb5-5d2b-ab8e-9ea0929edd9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317925} :answer-field/id #uuid "0be68def-e17f-5ecb-8c04-30bb05747fdd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d9aac98-3ca1-50d7-89f9-f776a2c7d658" :question/math-academy-id "q-348723" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-3\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2}({cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})-{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3}))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3})={cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})-{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2}({cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})-{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3}))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-3\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3})\\,{|}_{-3\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f309ede8-8cf6-5fe8-bd3b-f33334aed951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f309ede8-8cf6-5fe8-bd3b-f33334aed951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318374} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e89c1c2-350b-5b60-8d35-64c0b96418e2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "542465e2-8563-5f3e-ac3f-43f132e3b146" :question/math-academy-id "q-348724" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}(2{cos}^{2}⁡3x-2{sin}^{2}⁡3x+6x)\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x={cos}^{2}⁡3x-{sin}^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}(2{cos}^{2}⁡3x-2{sin}^{2}⁡3x+6x)\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+6x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+\\frac{6}{2}{x}^{2})|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+3{x}^{2})|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )+3{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}]-[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+3{(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}^{2}] \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319288 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61c02620-7e53-55de-8e0b-55604f5063bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}+\\frac{7\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317895} {:answer/id #uuid "878d0ada-2dc4-521b-bbd5-3ae173141b72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{2}-\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "878d0ada-2dc4-521b-bbd5-3ae173141b72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{2}-\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436656} :answer-field/id #uuid "4eb2c011-e2af-5007-a519-6733d9d7f018" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6c12a6bf-0854-5c5c-9be3-55c25edc3abf" :question/math-academy-id "q-348727" :question/problem "![](images/08/0817984c94a989cc5d0801c829e9c0479c7afb30223450faea46ec486b4494c6.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=-5{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})+5{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})+4x+7,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=0,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(-5{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})+5{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})+4x+7)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo calculate this integral, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x={cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite the above formula as\n\n$$\n-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-5{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})+5{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2}).\n$$\n\nHence, the area of the region is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(-5{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})+5{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})+4x+7)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4x+7)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{x}^{2}+7x)\\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =[-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+2(0)^{2}+7\\cdot 0]-[-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2{(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+7(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =0-[5+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}-\\frac{7\\pi }{2}] \\\\  & =-5-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}+\\frac{7\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319290 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "142a12aa-01b0-5072-9ca4-e1eec378309c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{96}+3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "142a12aa-01b0-5072-9ca4-e1eec378309c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{96}+3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317630} :answer-field/id #uuid "add3d045-7de3-5a9c-9515-587392b03ce7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ab13bb47-df00-5796-981e-179f6015730c" :question/math-academy-id "q-348730" :question/problem "![](images/fc/fc3c0c02be98834937150e6bee5a52ac14246bc128b189d3c095269f788d97a9.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=-4{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{x}^{2}+6,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(-4{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{x}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo calculate this integral, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x={cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite the above formula as\n\n$$\n-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x=-4{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nHence, the area of the region is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(-4{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{x}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+{x}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+6x)\\,{|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+\\frac{(\\pi /4)^{3}}{3}+6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}]-[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )+\\frac{(-\\pi /4)^{3}}{3}+6(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})] \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{192}+\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-(0-\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{192}-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{96}+3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4c4a965-e5c0-50b9-81a0-8bad3b7c2034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4c4a965-e5c0-50b9-81a0-8bad3b7c2034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318216} :answer-field/id #uuid "61980da2-3e36-5a2a-b4c4-3818d0774c84" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2f1fb432-4c99-5a83-93da-2a032802fa86" :question/math-academy-id "q-348731" :question/problem "![](images/02/020a73150209de3ee6f95fa1b21c85bd5673e500e4e421459eff90d2dd1a42e9.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=3{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})-3{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})+2x+1,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\pi ,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(3{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})-3{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo calculate this integral, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x={cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite the above formula as\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=3{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})-3{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2}).\n$$\n\nHence, the area of the region is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(3{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})-3{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{x}^{2}+x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+(\\pi )^{2}+\\pi ]-[3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+(0)^{2}+0] \\\\  & =[0+{\\pi }^{2}+\\pi ]-0 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319292 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9497cf1-df2a-5b21-ae7a-098af46ad402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\pi }{2}-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9497cf1-df2a-5b21-ae7a-098af46ad402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\pi }{2}-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318289} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dde6bb6-1669-5408-ab35-8a98f48c8709" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7cffec36-a2c7-52a4-899d-53e87b25a49f" :question/math-academy-id "q-348733" :question/problem "![](images/b9/b9a4695c6e43ee5ceb15ea1bbb3ee1815fdbd87e9c8b4a4e8eeba1089750ff79.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=6{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)-6{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+3x+17,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=0,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(6{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)-6{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+3x+17)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo calculate this integral, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x={cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite the above formula as\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x=6{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)-6{sin}^{2}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nHence, the area of the region is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(6{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)-6{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+3x+17)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+3x+17)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+17x)\\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =[\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+\\frac{3}{2}(0)^{2}+17\\cdot 0]-[\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )+\\frac{3}{2}{(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+17(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =[0+0+0]-[0+\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{8}-\\frac{17\\pi }{2}] \\\\  & =[0+\\frac{17\\pi }{2}-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{8}]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\pi }{2}-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319294 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55035a3b-5e59-557c-8a0d-6f859f28684a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55035a3b-5e59-557c-8a0d-6f859f28684a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317849} :answer-field/id #uuid "b609e088-9b4a-537a-8977-9eb139ccc5ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6453fac7-f808-5869-802f-ea2e359c84b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-348737" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(4{cos}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the expression $4{cos}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n4{cos}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2.\n$$\n\nThus, the integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(4{cos}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x{|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f9ed93-b649-5711-b87c-fa6c2f2360f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18f9ed93-b649-5711-b87c-fa6c2f2360f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317643} :answer-field/id #uuid "95806d21-5c36-5a2b-abb1-4115066bfc0c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a701d542-8f8a-544a-a009-39143886e3fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-348755" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e0a3fb0f4fc900f6ef8ac041ded7553f7ac44cc09f946bc3ebfee8a3235e8e94.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=5{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+1,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=0,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(5{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nRecall  ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1,$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+1 & =3{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2({sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x))+1 \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1.\n$$\n\nHence, the area of the region is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(3{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1)+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\frac{9}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\frac{9}{2}x)\\,|}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot 0)-(\\frac{3}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )+\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =(0+0)-(0-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a42441d-505c-5c53-95f5-9d1268223576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a42441d-505c-5c53-95f5-9d1268223576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317652} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b4fc236-a85f-5477-896f-2a51bd896757" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3af0a07e-3893-5b2a-b9da-b80cdbb6630d" :question/math-academy-id "q-348756" :question/problem "![](images/77/77004c9040818fbc254b9564b9c5821536ef65fcc77f6765b56d2e8524c24ed4.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=6{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})+2{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})+1,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ and $x=0,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-3\\pi /2}^{0}(6{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})+2{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nRecall  ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1,$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})+2{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})+1 & =4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})+2({sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})+{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3}))+1 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1.\n$$\n\nHence, the area of the region is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-3\\pi /2}^{0}(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3\\pi /2}^{0}(2(cos\\,(\\frac{2x}{3})+1)+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3\\pi /2}^{0}(2cos\\,(\\frac{2x}{3})+5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(3sin\\,(\\frac{2x}{3})+5x)\\,|}_{-3\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+5\\cdot 0)-(3sin\\,(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))+5(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =(0+0)-(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )-\\frac{15\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =(0)-(0-\\frac{15\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319297 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3a9e264-4112-5be7-b1ff-281b800e6d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3a9e264-4112-5be7-b1ff-281b800e6d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318322} :answer-field/id #uuid "4606941a-e383-5e6e-a067-fd55ab4bb95d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1cdb7061-4680-5ba2-8dc7-9dd171022ade" :question/math-academy-id "q-348766" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e40f35729a8e3df9f8403cad85bf0c4e6045733616c86cc399548a901c03ac7.png)\n\nA region in the $xy$-plane is bounded by the curve $y=2{cos}^{2}⁡x+x-1,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ as shown above. The area of the region is\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(2{cos}^{2}⁡x+x-1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate this integral, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(2{cos}^{2}⁡x+x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1)+x-1]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )+\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{2}]-[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b215b306-e35f-5003-9bdd-735f428b4839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b215b306-e35f-5003-9bdd-735f428b4839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318167} :answer-field/id #uuid "771bbe7e-2880-525c-873b-69188bad30d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f40df86-3936-5a06-ad55-95bff3a8fe95" :question/math-academy-id "q-348837" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}({cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2})-2{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}))\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall  ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ so letting $\\theta =\\frac{3x}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2})-2{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}) & =1-{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2})-2{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}) \\\\  & =1-3{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{3x}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))={sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}(1-3{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}(1-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{2}x)\\,|}_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}]-[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}] \\\\  & =[0-\\frac{\\pi }{3}]-[0-\\frac{\\pi }{6}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1632827-f0aa-5cb3-b052-1a7f817164df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1632827-f0aa-5cb3-b052-1a7f817164df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318316} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c6a8afd-da50-5710-bbdd-020fa158149f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0e74c108-0ed1-5674-9470-01c9cf8b9752" :question/math-academy-id "q-348844" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{3\\pi }^{6\\pi }6{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{6},$ we have\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})=2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{6}).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3\\pi }^{6\\pi }6{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{3\\pi }^{6\\pi }2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{3\\pi }^{6\\pi }[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{[x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})]}_{3\\pi }^{6\\pi } \\\\  & =3[6\\pi -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{3})-(3\\pi -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{3}))] \\\\  & =3[6\\pi -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-(3\\pi -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))] \\\\  & =3(6\\pi -3\\pi ) \\\\  & =9\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebd3353a-07d6-5c23-b181-74508647e944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebd3353a-07d6-5c23-b181-74508647e944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318353} :answer-field/id #uuid "694f826b-e296-5533-9a10-10841c8760fa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9c5e1c6b-1bd8-542e-a514-268b3f9603b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-348845" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-5\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}(8{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{5},$ we have\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5})=2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}(8{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-5\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}(4[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5})]+4x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}(4-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5})+4x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(4x-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5})+2{x}^{2})|}_{-5\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[4\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{2}-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2(5\\pi /2)}{5})+2{(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})}^{2}] \\\\  & \\,-[4\\cdot (-\\frac{5\\pi }{2})-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2(-5\\pi /2)}{5})+2{(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2})}^{2}] \\\\  & =[10\\pi -10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2(\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{4})]-[-10\\pi -10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )+2(\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{4})] \\\\  & =[10\\pi -0+\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{2}]-[-10\\pi -0+\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{2}] \\\\  & =20\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319301 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec3ba892-f906-5252-8d0a-fc753ca27c22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec3ba892-f906-5252-8d0a-fc753ca27c22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318355} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e68a549-1dbc-5d2a-94a9-f8e05540a280" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c872c2cf-5759-524f-829f-1674c73a940a" :question/math-academy-id "q-348849" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{8x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{8x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{8x}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{2}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{8x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{8x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{2}⁡x)(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{8})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{8})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{8}-2\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{8}-2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{8}-2I \\\\ 3I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{8} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{8x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{24}({ln}^{3}⁡x)\\,{|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}({ln}^{3}⁡({e}^{3})-{ln}^{3}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}((3\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{3}-{0}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}({3}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42412c56-59d1-5d81-93a2-a4f22e569838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=(1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{4}))\\,i+(2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{4}))\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317781} {:answer/id #uuid "4998624d-7db9-5275-90cc-85c0b0cbd843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=(1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8s))\\,i+(2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8s))\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317808} {:answer/id #uuid "acfc8104-37e4-50c2-bf09-f0cd5e873a66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=(1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2s))\\,i+(2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s))\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318152} {:answer/id #uuid "c89f6d96-4f48-59f9-9118-1bcd54a69aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=(1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))\\,i+(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318223} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e74fb8-0b30-5bb5-a2eb-7bf2759fc0e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=(1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))\\,i+(2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6e74fb8-0b30-5bb5-a2eb-7bf2759fc0e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=(1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))\\,i+(2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318380} :answer-field/id #uuid "d73adda4-e007-5b18-8007-737274149c7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cef1687d-4984-55ee-acf5-5623b63d8b7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-34885" :question/problem "Consider the curve$r(t)=(1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\,i+(2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,j,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by  $s=4t.$ Find $R(s),$ the parametrization of the curve by its arc length $s.$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $s=4t$, we have that $t=\\frac{s}{4}.$\n\nThe vector function that pametrizes the curve $r(t)$ by its arc length $s$, with $s\\ge 0$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{4}) \\\\  & =(1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{s}{4}))\\,i+(2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{s}{4}))\\,j \\\\  & =(1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))\\,i+(2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319303 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da3d4782-6eae-51f6-b535-97ff29bf9fc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da3d4782-6eae-51f6-b535-97ff29bf9fc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318293} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b4ff3de-182d-5b20-b76b-ea33007f45b5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f4f503db-530f-57b4-9e39-0ed91d9da902" :question/math-academy-id "q-348852" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }[6{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{4})-3+3cos\\,(\\frac{x}{2})+x]\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n6{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{4})=3(1-cos\\,(\\frac{x}{2})).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,\\,[6{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{4})-3+3cos\\,(\\frac{x}{2})+x]dx & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,\\,[3-3cos\\,(\\frac{x}{2})-3+3cos\\,(\\frac{x}{2})+x]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }x\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\pi )^{2}-{\\pi }^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319305 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d445613-d298-5d74-8ee1-4393889983c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d445613-d298-5d74-8ee1-4393889983c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317760} :answer-field/id #uuid "3518ba3d-c150-5fe9-a815-435bdad3b167" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0642e896-a4b3-54b6-8891-b6cfe0785402" :question/math-academy-id "q-348884" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}[4{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{6})-2+4cos\\,(\\frac{x}{3})]\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{6}$, we have\n\n$$\n4{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{6})=2(1-cos\\,(\\frac{x}{3})).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,\\,[4{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{6})-2+4cos\\,(\\frac{x}{3})]dx & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,\\,[2-2cos\\,(\\frac{x}{3})-2+4cos\\,(\\frac{x}{3})]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2cos\\,(\\frac{x}{3})\\,dx \\\\  & =6sin\\,(\\frac{x}{3}){|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =6[sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})] \\\\  & =6(1-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ec98abd-fe65-520a-8a2e-4e7d1cfc5d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{49}{48})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ec98abd-fe65-520a-8a2e-4e7d1cfc5d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{49}{48})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317950} :answer-field/id #uuid "4af6759a-4e3b-540c-bb7b-e531ae752551" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1545917f-fc9b-5e7b-a9c9-f1def306bc53" :question/math-academy-id "q-349040" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n12{x}^{2}-x-6=(4x-3)(3x+2).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6} & =\\frac{36x-10}{(4x-3)(3x+2)}=\\frac{A}{4x-3}+\\frac{B}{3x+2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n36x-10=A(3x+2)+B(4x-3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\frac{3}{4},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36(\\frac{3}{4})-10 & =A(3(\\frac{3}{4})+2)+B(4(\\frac{3}{4})-3) \\\\ 17 & =\\frac{17}{4}A \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{2}{3},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36(-\\frac{2}{3})-10 & =A(3(-\\frac{2}{3})+2)+B(4(-\\frac{2}{3})-3) \\\\ -34 & =-\\frac{17}{3}B \\\\ B & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}=\\frac{4}{4x-3}+\\frac{6}{3x+2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{4}{4x-3}+\\frac{6}{3x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x-3|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x-3|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(4x-3)(3x+2)^{2}|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(4x-3)(3x+2)^{2}|{|}_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{49}{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{49}{48}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{49}{48}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319317 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb6eb457-d510-5aac-a140-c028efa83fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{121}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb6eb457-d510-5aac-a140-c028efa83fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{121}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318239} :answer-field/id #uuid "080179ad-a368-58bf-b1b3-7a9fa9c592e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0a37242-8ca3-5cb0-9c32-a06114fb7f40" :question/math-academy-id "q-349041" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}-19x-4=(5x+1)(x-4).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4} & =\\frac{15x-39}{(5x+1)(x-4)}=\\frac{A}{5x+1}+\\frac{B}{x-4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n15x-39=A(x-4)+B(5x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(4)-39 & =A(4-4)+B(5(4)+1) \\\\ 21 & =21B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{5},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(-\\frac{1}{5})-39 & =A(-\\frac{1}{5}-4)+B(5(-\\frac{1}{5})+1) \\\\ -42 & =-\\frac{21}{5}A \\\\ A & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}=\\frac{10}{5x+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{10}{5x+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(5x+1)^{2}(x-4)|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(5x+1)^{2}(x-4)|{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(242)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{242}{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{121}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
